WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.890
Parsing and validation solve different problems.

2
00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:06.586
Parsing converts serialized bytes or text into a data structure.

3
00:00:06.586 --> 00:00:11.022
Validation checks whether that structure matches expected rules and
intent.

4
00:00:11.022 --> 00:00:24.732
A parser may accept a document because the syntax is valid, while the
application should still reject it because required fields are missing,
types are wrong, values are out of range, relationships are invalid, or
the operation is not allowed.

5
00:00:24.732 --> 00:00:32.125
Schema validation should define expected fields, required fields, allowed
fields, types, ranges, formats, and relationships.

6
00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:37.367
For example, a message may require an identifier, a timestamp, an action,
and a list of items.

7
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:43.282
Validation should decide whether unknown fields are rejected, ignored, or
allowed for compatibility.

8
00:00:43.282 --> 00:00:54.304
That decision should be intentional because unexpected fields can indicate
client error, version mismatch, probing, or an attempt to control behavior
the application did not intend to expose.

9
00:00:54.304 --> 00:00:57.530
Type normalization should happen before sensitive use.

10
00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:08.014
Strings may need trimming, identifiers may need canonical form, numbers
may need range checks, timestamps may need timezone handling, and
enumerations may need exact allowed values.

11
00:01:08.014 --> 00:01:11.710
Normalization should reduce ambiguity, not hide invalid input.

12
00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:17.087
If a field can mean too many things, downstream code becomes harder to
secure and harder to test.

13
00:01:17.087 --> 00:01:21.523
Validation should happen close to the trust boundary and produce safe
errors.

14
00:01:21.523 --> 00:01:25.958
Developers and legitimate clients need enough information to correct
input.

15
00:01:25.958 --> 00:01:33.082
Unauthorized users should not receive secrets, stack traces, internal
paths, parser internals, or configuration details.

16
00:01:33.082 --> 00:01:41.080
Strong parsing and validation make failure modes predictable, reviewable,
and testable before deserialized data reaches sensitive code.
